  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 220 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nCurrent satellite trends show fog and lower clouds are lifting \nout of Southwest MT as warmer air mixes down to the surface. \nAcross North-Central MT, patchy dense fog has lifted but lower \nclouds have been slower to dissipate. \n\nAn upper-level trough bringing a low pressure system moves into\nthe region later today, bringing precipitation from the southwest\nto the north. Warm air advection ahead of the system will bring \nrain initially, but a cold front will lower snow levels, \ntransitioning to light snow in the mountains. Snow levels do fall\ntowards 4,000ft to 4,500ft Tuesday morning, bringing a light rain\nto snow mix at lower elevations. \n\nThroughout the week, multiple quick hitting troughs move through\nthe northwest, keeping daily chances for precipitation through \nthe rest of the week and into the beginning of next week. \nPrecipitation will be mainly in the mountains/passes with light \nsnow, especially along the northern Continental Divide. Isolated \nrain/snow will be possible at lower elevations at times throughout\nthe week. Cooler temperatures remain through the forecast period \ndue to the consistent troughing pattern.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nDense fog and lower clouds are lifting out of Southwest MT this \nafternoon as the warm front approaches. Therefore, I will allow\nthe dense fog advisory to expire at 3pm. Any lingering fog this\nafternoon will become mixed out later in the day as the incoming \nsystem approaches.\n\nMessaging remains consistent with this incoming system today into\nTuesday. There's a 30% chance for 3\" of snow or greater above\n~6,000ft in Southwest MT and Marias Pass, keeping minor impacts\nfor snow at pass and above pass level. Additional accumulations \nlater this week look to remain light, with Marias Pass at a 30% \nchance again for 3\" of snow on Wednesday. Wet bulb temperatures \nstruggle to reach 32 degrees across lower elevations except for \nthe higher Southwest MT Valleys, therefore any snow that does fall\nall lower elevations will struggle to accumulate. -Wilson",
